Newsgroups: alt.politics.libertarian
Subject: Competitive, non-territorial defense services

Somebody wrote:
>[If you have more property] You have more to insure, so your premiums 
>are higher...

Here "insurance" is used to justify collecting income taxes to
fund militaries.  Instead of just using insurance as a metaphor
why not take it seriously and raise the money through voluntary premiums 
instead of taxes?  Actually why not divide defense of people and 
property into two parts:

(1) Policies that insure against the failure of defense.
Currently, most life and property insurance policies won't pay out in cases 
of riot, war, etc. because the high damage claims cannot be distributed 
widely enough throughout society (a large war would bankrupt the insurance 
companies).  But by hedging policies with catastrophe futures to spread risk 
throughout the futures market (which would rise and fall based on 
prospects for war destruction), the insurance companies could handle 
such large payouts.

(2) Voluntary fees paid to chosen military services (more on this below).

Insurance companies regulate military services by charging
higher fees to customers who retain inferior defense services.

Is there any reason why modern militaries must have service
area monopolies, aka territorial countries?  In an era where boundaries 
are unimportant to most threats (ships, planes, missiles, 
satellites, terrorists, etc.) and we often find a potpourri of military
units from dozens of nations grouped together as "peacekeepers" in various 
world hotspots, why not open defense services up to competitive bid on a 
per household basis?  Of course the military services will still 
own non-contiguous pieces of property for their own use (bases), still 
contract out to global arms makers, etc. but there's not much reason left
for them to retain territorial monopolies for providing defense services.
There is more justification for cable companies and electric utilities 
to have territorial monopolies, than there is for modern militaries.

Intermingling of competitive forces, and the incentive of insurers
to write contract terms that minimize the probability and destructiveness
of war, decreases the chance of full-scale war breaking out.  Attacks 
from territorial countries can be repulsed when the intermingled 
competitors ally to fulfill their defense contracts, while insurance 
contracts and the futures market allow insurers and customers to regulate 
and monitor how well the defense services fulfill their contracts.  
Obviously each service will favor their own customers, but any detrimental 
impact this might have on the course of the war can be mitigated by 
cross-payment of services fees.  Also, such rivalry need be no worse than 
rivalry between allies and services branches during traditional wars 
between territorial countries.


