Subject: Inventions & concepts

[ Ratha and Freeman Craig Prsson discuss concepts and inventions in
  non-human animals.]
 
     There are some references on cognition in other animals which might
be of interest here (none on squirrels -- I promise).  _The_Education_of_
_Koko_, by Francine Patterson and Eugene Linden (Holt, Rinehart and Winston,
1981) describes the first decade of _Gorilla_gorilla_ Koko's exposure to
learning and using American Sign Language.  At age ten, Koko's cognitive
processing and use of sign language appear to have been broadly comparable
to those of a _Homo_sapiens_ three-year-old.  Koko's behavior was
sufficiently suggestive of symbolic reasoning to give a solid shake to the
cages of the rather diverse group of scholars who didn't believe she ought
to be able to do that.  There's a lot of material here -- I will give one
example, from page 209 -- italics are in the original, used to indicate
sign-language signs -- the experimenter is both speaking and signing (and
Koko's understanding of spoken English was on a par with her understanding
of signs -- several hundred words regularly well-used, several hundred
more used correctly at least once):
 
    When I said to her, "_After_ you _kiss_ your _baby_ [doll],
    I'll _give-you_ these _leaves_," Koko turned, hunted for the
    doll and found it on the other side of the room, kissed it,
    and returned to me, hand outstretched for the leaves.
 
That sounds a lot like simple application of the concept "contract".
 
    Ken Norris, in _Dolphin_Days_ (Norton, 1991) chronicles and summarizes
his career studying small cetaceans.  Pages 46-48 describe how _Steno_
_bredanensis_ (rough-toothed dolphin) Malia, in a marine park in Hawaii,
quickly learned to provide _new_ behaviors.  His conclusion was that
"Malia had understood this need for something _new_."
 
    John Bonner's _The_Evolution_of_Culture_in_Animals_ (Princeton, 1980)
is a thoughtful survey of means whereby animals deal with information not
encoded in their genome, from bacteria on up.  The ultimate focus here was
on passing information from one individual to another -- notably, from
generation to generation.  My copy is on loan so I can't cite specific
examples, but there are lots of them, in small increments of complexity,
from insects up through primates.  (Bacteria are discussed in the context
of how a bit or two of short-term memory about chemical stimulii can
provide a useful swimming strategy; namely, "if things are getting worse,
swim randomly, otherwise (notably if things are getting better) swim in
a straight line" -- which works if the reason things are getting better
is because you had finally, randomly selected a "good" direction to
swim in.)
 
    I've got more, if anyone is interested.
                                                 -- Jay Freeman

