"Boy, I admire you, Phred!"

It is regrettable that English doesn't seem to have a verb meaning "want
possessions identical to those of".  "Covet" takes as its direct object the
possessions themselves, not the person.  ("I covet your car, Phred!", not
"I covet you, Phred!")

English thus seems to have a built-in zero-sum orientation... the concept
of wanting something of someone else's without begrudging them the
continued possession of it takes (as you see) an inordinate number of words
to express.  But, fortunately, English is easily extensible.  We can:

1) Borrow an existing word with the correct meaning from another language
(anybody know of such a word)?

2) Coin a new word ("Boy, I fbzlt you, Phred!")

3) Overload an existing word.  I think letting "covet" take an indirect
object is the way to go.  "Boy, I covet you your car, Phred!"

