Date: Wed, 15 Sep 1993 23:03:58 +0000 (GMT)
From: fwp@fwpam.charlotte.nc.us (Rick Pasotto)
Subject: EPIS: Sensory evidence

Rather than make several inidividual replies, I'll try to make one
coherent reply. This may result in excerpts from a single message
appearing separated and out of sequence (I hope not out of context).

The epistemology I will be explaining is objectivist as I understand 
it. A major part of this understanding comes from the set of tapes by 
David Kelley "Foundations of Knowledge: An Objectivist Perspective", 
available from Laissez Faire Books. 

DD -> pierce.ee.washington.edu!davisd (Dan Davis)
DK -> synopsys.com!dkrieger (Dave Krieger)
DZ -> cs.wisc.edu!derek (Derek Zahn)
PM -> "Perry E. Metzger" <lehman.com!pmetzger>

As is frequently the case in debates most of the heat comes from
differing word usages, both absolutely and in precision. Perry alludes
to the problem when he writes:

PM> English phrases can possess multiple interpretations --  some advocate
PM> the use of E-prime to encourage enough precision of expression that
PM> such ambiguities cease to be a problem. (I tried to phrase that in
PM> E-Prime -- perhaps someone can say it better?)

But changing languages will not solve the problem. A better approach
is to constantly ask yourself "why does my oppenent think the way he
does?" and "are my words as clear as I can make them?" In other words,
think before you post. It also helps to assume an honest search for 
knowledge on all sides.

PM> DD> I ask, however, how does "sensory evidence is ALWAYS valid" mean
PM> DD> "that which I do not model does not exist"?
PM> 
PM> People might overinterpret that way.
PM> 
PM> As for the particular question of how someone might draw the inference
PM> I stated -- the overgeneralization might be 
PM> 
PM> 1) sensory data is always valid
PM> 2) I don't sense a bus
PM> 3) Therefore, there is no bus about to hit me
PM> 
PM> Of course the correct chain is
PM> 
PM> 1) sensory data appears to be valid most of the time
PM> 2) I don't sense a bus
PM> 3) Therefore, from what I can tell thus far, no bus is about to
PM>    hit me.

The above quote is a very good example of the miscommunication we're
having. My response to this is that it is impossible to "sense a bus".
Our eyes can sense the energy of certain wavelengths of light, our
ears (and perhaps skin) can sense changes in the air pressure, our
nose can sense certain chemicals in the air, our skin can sense
changes in the ambient temperature. But none of these individually or
as a group is a "bus".

Our nervous system then takes these sensations and "groups" them into
perceptions. It is these perceptions we are aware of. So far this
process is entirely mechanical. Light of a specific wavelength enters
the eye and results in certain physical reactions that are physically
processed by our nervous system. This is what an objectivist means when
he says the evidence of the senses is "valid". In other words, from 
"reality" to "perception" is a causal chain completely determined by 
the identity of each aspect of the chain.

What happens next, when our consciousness processes the perceptions,
is where the trouble starts. It is only here that it is meaningful to
talk of a "bus". Thus an objectivist would say:

    1) My analysis of my perceptions is (usually, mostly, 99.99% --
       depending on the situation) accurate
    2) I do not have any perceptions that would indicate a bus
    3) Therefore, from what I can tell thus far, no bus is about to 
       hit me.

DK> Tim and Rick Pasotto are redefining "perception" to exclude the act of
DK> interpretation which is implicit in the conventional definition of the
DK> word.  (Dictionaries provide two kinds of definitions for "perception":
DK> the common usage is defined as synonymous with "cognition", while the
DK> technical definition in the field of psychology explicitly identifies
DK> "perception" as "an awareness" of a stimulus.)

If "stimulus" is understood to be a "bundle" of sensations, then the
technical definition corresponds to my usage. My Random House 
Unabridged gives: "A single unified awareness derived from sensory 
processes while a stimulus is present."

DK> "Perceptual judgement" is "perception"; there is no "perception"
DK> independent of the perceiver's evaluation of incoming sense data.

The evaluation by the consciousness is separate from the
pre-processing of the nervous system.

PM> DZ> I haven't been following it too closely -- but if somebody is
PM> DZ> really  claiming that our senses reveal an objective reality
PM> DZ> unerringly, that's clearly hooey.
PM> 
PM> I don't think that claim has yet been specifcally made in this thread,
PM> but it is not hooey.
PM>
PM> Oh? Try taking some LSD sometime and continue to make the argument.

This is as useful (or useless) a response as it would be to respond to
a statement about what happens when two billiard balls collide by
saying "what if one of the balls was a meat ball?" In other words,
totally irrelevant. If you alter the processor you should expect to
get different outputs from similar inputs.

If one bit in a computer memory chip were stuck as a zero you may not 
always get the results you want, but you can at least detect and 
probably cope with it. However if the chip randomly flipped bits it 
would be useless. Our sense organs and nervous system have evolved 
because they work sufficiently well to be useful. Where is your 
evidence that they are unreliable?

PM> Rick Pasotto says:
PM> > The essential point of the argument is that the vatted-brain/VR would 
PM> > have to exist in "the real world"; and to ensure absolutely no 
PM> > discrepancies (ie, contradictions) between the VR and the "real world" 
PM> > would require omniscience and omnipotence.
PM> 
PM> The point is not to simulate the real world perfectly, but to simulate
PM> the input to the senses perfectly enough that the vatted brain cannot
PM> tell the difference. You needn't get the Taj Mahal absolutely right --
PM> just good enough that the vatted brain doesn't know it isn't in the
PM> Taj Mahal. This requires neither omniscience nor ominipotence. I've
PM> already explained those arguments thoroughly.

How do you know how good is good enough? Just one little slip and it's 
now *possible* to detect a difference. You would not only have to know 
every thought, but also anticipate every thought. Part of the evidence 
we perceive is how our actions relate to the outside world. If we move 
our hand across our field of vision, not only is what we see changed 
but also our perception of how our hand feels (eg. its motion).

I realize it is very easy for you to posit an ever bigger (or is it 
smaller?) and faster computer. But no matter what the computer, you 
would have to more than duplicate the thinking of the vatted brain.
In order to avoid the need for omniscience you would have to take over 
the brain's thinking, at which point the example becomes moot. (You 
might be able to get away with it if the brain had an IQ of 60 or so; 
but if you can posit an ever better computer, I can posit an ever 
greater intelligence.)

PM> However, to hold ANYTHING "unequivocally" is the real denial of reality.

I wonder, does Perry hold to this statement unequivocally?

PM> Infinite regress has to end somewhere.

Wonderful! Care to define infinite? How about: without end.

PM> The point is that Kelley, Rand, and that whole group are rather upset
PM> when philosophers in the tradition of Decartes say "the real world is
PM> unknowable in an absolute sense because you can't know that your sense
PM> data is aimed at the real world or is being produced by a 'deceiver'".

What does it mean to say "unknowable in an absolute sense"? Perry talks
as if he absolutely knows that the world is absolutely unknowable.  This
is the same kind of error as asking when time began.

PM> Rand and Company spent lots of time and energy railing against this.

Yes, it's this kind of self-contradictory babble that objectivism objects
to.

Rick

